# NightSeam Web - response security headers for the /astro deployment.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: the desktop shell ships a strict Content-Security-Policy
# in `app/server.py` (CSP_HEADER), but the web shell is served as static files,
# so it had NO policy at all - and it is the shell on a PUBLIC origin, loading
# third-party images and running the user's stored data in the same context. A
# single XSS there reaches localStorage, IndexedDB (sites/rigs/targets/panoramas)
# and every key the user owns.
#
# `script-src` is 'self' + the SHA-256 of the ONE inline theme bootstrap in
# `index.html` - deliberately NOT 'unsafe-inline'. That script only applies the
# saved theme before first paint; if the hash ever goes stale the worst case is
# a one-frame flash of the default theme, never a broken app.
# `tests/browser/harness.py::_csp_problems` recomputes the hash and fails
# `python scripts/check_browser.py --prod-url https://nightseam.de/astro/` if it
# does not match, so drift is detected rather than shipped.
#
# MEASURED LIMIT OF THIS FILE (2026-09-15): on Plesk the static files are
# fronted by NGINX, which serves .html/.js/.css itself, so these directives
# arrive only on the paths nginx proxies (/astro/version.json does; /astro/
# and /astro/app.js do NOT). Covering the HTML needs the same header at the
# nginx layer (Plesk > domain > Apache & nginx Settings > Additional nginx
# directives), e.g. `add_header Content-Security-Policy` with the same
# value as the Header line below, marked `always`.
#
# BUG-003 (2026-09-17): this is now a HARD FAILURE of the production smoke, not
# a note. `python scripts/check_browser.py --prod-url https://nightseam.de/astro/`
# reads the policy from the PAGE response ONLY and fails when it is absent: a
# policy on version.json/catalog.manifest.json protects nothing, because a CSP
# covers the response that carries it and those are not the HTML a browser runs.
# The nginx directive to add is the Header value below (`add_header
# Content-Security-Policy "<that value>" always;` at the `/astro` location).
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-17, why the header below is NOT the live protection: /astro/
# answers `server: nginx` + `content-type: text/html` with no policy header, i.e.
# nginx serves the HTML itself and never reaches Apache - and this host exposes no
# nginx directive field. The enforced policy therefore ships as a
# `<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">` in web/index.html, carrying every
# directive below EXCEPT `frame-ancestors` (ignored in a meta policy, so it is
# deliberately omitted there). If the host ever gains the directive field, add the
# line below and the header simply becomes the stronger of the two.
#
# `style-src 'unsafe-inline'` is REQUIRED, not a convenience relaxation: index.html
# styles several controls with inline `style=` attributes (range widths, theme
# swatches) and `style-src 'self'` blocks them SILENTLY - measured 2026-09-17,
# Chrome logged no "Refused to" line and the page still booted; only the sliders
# and swatches lost their styling. The `web-prod` check measures that at runtime
# now (the green swatch must actually be green).
#
# mod_headers must be enabled (Plesk: Apache with mod_headers - the default).
# If this file causes a 500, the module is off: remove the file rather than
# leaving a broken site.

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-MuhKQH9PAUOpe+xRJwpI/rHBZrPBvgVXN9NSrMvuoqY='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://upload.wikimedia.org; connect-src 'self' https://en.wikipedia.org https://api.open-meteo.com https://air-quality-api.open-meteo.com; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'"
Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
</IfModule>
